  "answer_text": "**Yes, early versions of Coca-Cola did contain cocaine.** \n\nThe history of how cocaine ended up in the drink, how much was actually in it, and how it was eventually removed is a fascinating story of 19th-century medicine and clever marketing.\n\n### 1. The Origin of the Recipe\nCoca-Cola was invented in **1885** by an Atlanta pharmacist named John Stith Pemberton. At the time, cocaine was completely legal, unregulated, and widely considered a \"wonder drug\". Pemberton, who was a morphine addict seeking a cure for his own addiction, initially created a beverage called *Pemberton's French Wine Coca*. \n\nWhen Atlanta passed local prohibition laws in 1886, he reformulated the drink into a non-alcoholic syrup. He named his new creation **Coca-Cola** after its two primary active ingredients:\n*   **\"Coca\"** from the Peruvian coca leaf (which naturally contains cocaine).\n*   **\"Cola\"** from the African kola nut (which contains caffeine, another stimulant).\n\nIt was marketed as a \"patent medicine\" and brain tonic designed to cure headaches, fatigue, and depression.\n\n### 2. How Much Cocaine Was in It?\nWhile early Coca-Cola did contain cocaine, it was not an overwhelming amount. \n\nHistorians estimate that a typical glass of Coca-Cola in the late 1880s contained about **9 milligrams of cocaine**. For comparison, a standard recreational \"line\" of cocaine typically contains between 50 to 75 milligrams. Drinking a Coca-Cola would have provided a mild, stimulating \"buzz\"\u2014similar to a strong cup of coffee today\u2014rather than an intense, drug-like high.\n\n### 3. When Was Cocaine Removed?\nBy the late 1890s, public opinion on cocaine began to shift dramatically. Society was growing increasingly aware of the dangers of drug addiction, and racial and social panic in the American South further demonized the drug. \n\nIn response to growing pressure and fear of government regulation, the head of Coca-Cola, Asa Candler, began reducing the amount of cocaine in the recipe. \n*   By **1903**, the formula was altered so that the cocaine was almost entirely eliminated.\n*   By **1929**, scientists perfected the process of using \"spent\" (completely decocainized) coca leaves. From this point forward, the beverage became 100% cocaine-free.\n\n### 4. How Coca-Cola is Made Today\nIn a surprising twist, **Coca-Cola still uses coca leaves to flavor the drink today**. \n\nBecause the exact flavor profile of the coca leaf is critical to the top-secret \"7X\" recipe, the Coca-Cola Company has a special arrangement with the U.S. government. \n\nA chemical processing company in Maywood, New Jersey, called the **Stepan Company**, is the only facility in the United States legally authorized to import and process coca leaves. Stepan Company imports the leaves from South America, chemically extracts the active cocaine, and sells that cocaine to pharmaceutical companies for medical use (such as local anesthetics). The leftover \"decocainized\" coca leaf extract is then sent to Coca-Cola factories to be used as a flavoring agent.",
